        <h3 id="analytical-introduction">Analytical Introduction <a href="#analytical-introduction" class="anchor">🔗</a></h3>
<p>In what follows I offer two examples of how this edition can be used as a resource for literary studies. The first will investigate the essay’s material conditions and the ways this materiality inflects and/or reflects its concepts. The second study will demonstrate how the encoded XML can be utilized for CLS analyses of the fluid text.</p>
<p><b>Material (A)symmetry: Art, Nature, and The Mirror</b></p>
<p>In an essay concerned with discussing and inverting the relationships between Originality and Imitation, Art and Nature—or as phrased in a revised passage “Art finds her own perfection within, and not outside of, herself. She is not to be judged by any external standard of <s>perfection</s> <b>resemblance</b>. She is a veil, rather than a mirror.” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f032/f/f032/folio31_fluid/-1/g" target="_blank">f.31</a>)—it may come as no surprise that such mirroring, and problematizing of this mirroring, surfaces on and through the material MS as well. Indeed, I suggest, the aesthetic theory proffered in the essay, deeply concerned with the intangibility of individual subjectivity and the imagination, has, for Wilde, material foundations and implications for our understanding of the material word that are legible and reified in the material conditions of inscription.</p> 
<p>A key contention of Vivian’s “article” recited in the dialogue is that Art supersedes, precedes, and contains exceeds nature (see Danson, Green, Riquelme, Small, and Sussman). In Vivian’s schema (and Wilde’s if we take Vivian as a mouthpiece), Art is not to be reduced to a mirror of nature, but rather, at its pinnacle Art inverts the conventional art-nature mirror relationship. Motifs of reflection and representation (of mirroring) by art, the “self-conscious” artist, life, and nature are both the rhetorical crux and general thesis of many of the essay’s critical assertions. Moreover, the art-nature mirror dynamic that predominates the conceptual focus of the text are reified and reflected in the material condition of the MS, tangibly mirroring or echoing the aesthetic concerns of the dialogue on the very pages they were inscribed. For example, on <a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f018/f/f018/folio17_fluid/-1/g?zone=%23f055_z001">f.17</a>, Wilde wrote for Vivian:</p> 
<div class="callout callout-quote"><blockquote>The public<br>imagine that, because they are interested<br>in their immediate surroundings,  Art<br><i>should</i> be interested in them also, and<br><i>should</i> take them as her subject-<br>matter. But the mere fact that they<br>are interested in these things makes<br>them unsuitable subjects for Art.</blockquote></div>
<img src="https://i.imgur.com/Oi9N9FX.jpeg" style="max-width:100%; height:auto; display:block; margin:0 auto;" alt="f.17 of The Decay of Lying">
<p style="text-align: center;">Figure 1. f.17 of “The Decay of Lying,” University of Akron: Herman Muehlstein Rare Book Collection. PR5818.d43 1889</p>
<p>In a critique of the popular sense of what Art <i>should</i> do—that art’s “interest” and “subject-matter” <i>should</i> mirror the interest of “the public” and their “immediate-surroundings” in its content—Wilde, creating a semantic redundancy for emphasis, reiterates the word “should.” But he does so by stacking the terms, one on top of the other, literally mirroring them over the line break. While in any printed edition the repetition works to drive home to reiterative nature of such Art, in the MS this emphasis is materially amplified. The conceptual discussion of art as a mirroring representation of public “interest” and life is materially re-instantiated and reinscribed, materially mirrored, in the very construction of the sentence.</p> 
<p>While such moments occur sporadically in the MS,<sup><a href="#note-3" id="ref-note-3">3</a></sup> the dynamic mirroring motif—Wilde’s complication of the relationship between art as representation of nature and/or life—arises in its most exemplary material instantiation on <a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f031/f/f031/folio30_fluid/-1/g">f.30</a>. On this folio Wilde wrote:</p> 
<div class="callout callout-quote"><blockquote>To excuse themselves<br>they will try and shelter under the<br>shield of him who made Prospero<br>the magician, and gave him Caliban<br>and Ariel to serve him as his servants, who<br>heard the Tritons blowing their horns<br>round the coral reefs of the Enchanted<br>Isle.</blockquote></div>
<p>The mirroring here is even less visible to the reader of any single reading-text edition, or even the <i>Akron</i> manuscript, than the line break symmetry above. As Josephine Guy reported in 2014, f19 of the <i>Berg </i> MS is actually from the fair-copy of a review for Wyke Bayliss’s The Enchanted Isle (titled “The New President” and is quite critical), which came out the same month as “Decay of Lying.” Both writings carry allusions/direct references to Shakespeare’s <i>The Tempest</i>, forming the basis for her argument that Wilde’s notorious “Self-Plagiarism[s]” may actually be a result of complex drafting process where multiple drafts of the same and different pieces were being composed simultaneously. This interleaving of material may be what results in the extensive reusing&#x27;s of whole passages (like the discussion of Balzac on <a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f017/f/f017/folio16_fluid/-1/g">f.16</a> that is almost entirely lifted from his review published a few years prior), and it suggests an interestingly recursive composition process; a drafting process that would account for the initial reference to Caliban above that was replaced in the 1891 variant.  This interleaving of texts materially instantiates the mirror motif, with mirroring allusions arising from a reflection of an original inscription. But in its existence, it complicates the art-nature relationship. Here, it is art that mirrors art, creating a materially and textually self-reflexive literature—a “mode of writing” or “style” (Wilde replaces “style” on the page)—that resembles the “imitation” Wilde critiques on this page (<i>Berg</i> f19). But rather than imitate another, which prevents achieving a “great literary style,” it is a mirroring of his own thought inscribed, a distilling reflection of a material from of his own subjectivity that resembles Pater’s notion of “style” (<i>Berg</i> f19).  Moreover, even when the mirroring effect is physically displaced and obscured through inscription and printing across different materials, its nature as texts gives the mirror, and art and subjectivity as what is mirrored, a temporal permanence that, in Wildes view (as discussed below), stifles endeavors to render art the mirror of life. </p>
<p>And beyond purely documentary features, what has gone unexplored is how the latter portion of this short piece alludes to ideas about the role and purpose of art, quintessentially decadent notions, that Wilde teases out in “The Decay of Lying.” For example, Wilde remarks that:</p>
<div class="callout callout-quote"><blockquote>the pictures of contemporary events, Royal marriages naval reviews and things of this kind, that appear in the Academy every year, are always extremely bad, while the very same subjects treated in black and white in the <i>Graphic</i> or the <i>London News</i> are excellent. Besides if we want to understand the history of a nation <b>through the medium of art</b> <i>it is to the imaginative and ideal arts that we have to go, and not to the arts that are definitely imitative. The visible aspect of life no longer contains for us the secret of life’s spirit. Probably it never did contain </i>(3:3).</blockquote></div><p>The review (and many of his reviews Green argues) conceptually mirror the ideas that “true” art emerges from the “imaginative,” i.e., “lying,” and linguistically overlaps with the revised assertion on f.43 and f.44: “The more abstract, the more ideal an art is, the more it reveals to us the temper of its age. If we wish to understand a nation <b>by means of its art, </b> let us look at its architecture <s>and</s> <b>or</b> its music.” What’s more, the idea of an “imaginative” realism echoes his praise of Balzac in both his review of <i>Lost Illusions</i>, and, in repurposed fashion, in the “Decay of Lying” (delivered via a critique of Zola). Such an example of conceptual resonances nicely exemplifies Van Hulle’s notion of “creative concurrence” and helps us understand the work as always becoming and inherently “social”—in conversation with others and with our past ideas. </p> 
<p>Wilde’s writing in this recursive and reiterative fashion, in which he lifts and recontextualizes whole passages, may be a self-reflexive form of the type of “creative synthesis” (Bristow and Mitchell) or “absorption” and “communication with style” (Schiff) that enacts the aesthetic theory Schiff reads in the essay (or a necessity when writing so prolifically). It is not “imitative” but a creative material and conceptual self-reflexivity, creating something new out of the same refashioned material/constitutive parts. Moreover, it demonstrates that textual fluidity is not bound to or by a single work-concept. It extends, as Van Hulle shows, throughout (and even outside, Bryant might argue) a whole <i>oeuvre</i>—moving across discrete “texts” but surfacing on (or through) the same material—materially reifying “creative concurrence.” Ideas are articulated, rearticulated, reimagined amidst new contexts, even if the words are largely the same.</p> 
<p> Indeed, the mirroring of art in art, from one side of the material text to another, remains legible across materials and frames the subsequent expressions and our interactions with art objects. The material mirroring of content goes beyond a figurative bleeding through, but erupts in content of the passage that follows as Wilde complicates the Art-Nature mirroring dynamic in his reading and interpretation of Shakespeare:</p>
<div class="callout callout-quote"><blockquote>They will call upon Shakespeare—<br>they always do—and will quote that<br>hackneyed passage about Art holding<br>the mirror up to Nature, forgetting that<br>this unfortunate aphorism is deliberately<br>said by Hamlet in order to convince<br>the bystanders of his absolute<br>insanity in all art matters (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f031/f/f031/folio30_fluid/-1/g" target="_blank">f.31</a>)</blockquote></div>
<p>And it is perhaps no coincidence that on the following folio of the <i>Akron</i> MS, Vivian declares that Art “is not to be judged by any external standard of resemblance” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f032/f/f032/folio31_fluid/-1/g" target="_blank">f.31</a>). The materiality of Wilde’s “self-plagiarisms” converse with the essay’s aesthetic principles to both articulate and demonstrate an aesthetic schema, in which Art does not (re)mediate nature. Rather, “the medium of art” is a structure and structuring force, creating and acting as the framework for understanding our impressions and perceptions of nature (Schiff 90). Our encounters with art create the structure of “beauty” that then frames our perceptions and aesthetic judgements of nature, which are in turn, according to Kantian aesthetics, taken as inherent qualities of the object (nature) itself (what Kant terms “subreption”). And it is this understanding of art that Vivian proffers when he asserts, that art “is a veil, rather than a mirror.” Art does not reflect beauty in nature but creates the images that we take for a mirror (in ways we do not even recognize). Hence, too, why “The more abstract, the more ideal an art is, the more it reveals to us the temper of its age” and that great art anticipates life (f.43). Finally, this dynamic becomes visible, as Wilde’s essay demonstrates, in moments of artistic self-reflexivity (mirroring) and the conceptual and material (a)symmetry that arises. Our own subjective positions, and the way art and subjectivity inflect what we assume to be objective, are hyper-legible when art physically and conceptually structures and mirrors itself.</p>
<p>Of course, in Wildean and decadent fashion, Vivian’s seemingly paradoxical aesthetic schema does not remain unproblematic nor straightforward. In the MS and <i>Nineteenth Century</i> variants, Wilde would go on to reference Caliban once more, but ultimately revised out the allusion for the 1891 publication:</p>
<div class="callout callout-quote"><blockquote>his work is entirely wrong<br>from beginning to end, and wrong<br>not on the ground of morals, but on<br>the ground of Art. From any ethical<br>standpoint it is just what it<br>should be. The author is perfectly truthful, and<br>describes things exactly as they happen.<br>What more can any moralist desire?<br>We have no sympathy at all with<br>the moral indignation of our time against<br>M. Zola. It is simply the rage indignation of
<br><br><b>Tartuffe on being exposed</b> <s>Caliban on seeing his own face in a<br>glass</s> (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f013/f/f013/folio12_fluid/-1/g" target="_blank">f.12</a>)</blockquote></div>
<p>Wilde changes the allusion from <i>The Tempest</i> to Molière’s <i>Le Tartuff</i> when revising for <i>Intentions</i>. Guy notes that “In <i>De Profundis</i> and a cancelled draft of <i>A Woman of No Importance</i> Wilde cites both characters as interchangeable,” yet, here at least, Caliban’s anger emerges out of self-reflection, a literal looking into the mirror, whereas Tartuffe is exposed by others. One is firmly grounded in an inward-looking act, while the other external. In the dichotomy the essay has crafted, art <i>should</i> expose the self, holding up a mirror to the individuality of subjective experience in an act of self-reflexive, creative “lying” in contrast to an externally focused, mimetic (in someways, Wilde argues, more imitative and deceptive) realism (see Schiff 87). So, while the material allusions are mirrored across discrete texts with shared materials, over the course of the work's life the mirroring allusions are ultimately erased by the texts fluidity (You cannot step into the same river twice, so to speak), further complicating the art-nature mirroring relationship.</p>
<p><b>Defining, Refining, Amplifying the Criticism: Mapping Additions, Deletions, and Sentiment</b></p>
<p>Parsing the text by stage using the encoded XML allows for visualization of any version of the text, as well as analysis of words added and deleted. For example, below is a terms table for deleted words across all stages, as well as a map charting the stage when the most frequent of these words were removed.</p> 
<div style="text-align: center;">
  <iframe style='width: 677px; height: 523px;' src='https://voyant-tools.org/tool/CorpusTerms/?stopList=keywords-c0890b0bcac44ce34a40d6c6872d9506&comparisonCorpus=&corpus=be075b0e15e2f86da6c8275a7f73c2ac'></iframe>
</div>
<img src="https://i.imgur.com/3jT1ku5.jpeg" style="max-width:100%; height:auto; display:block; margin:0 auto;" alt="Deleted words by Stage Heatmap">

<p>The frequent deletions of the word “art” suggests that for all the priority Wilde gives to art, as the essay changed, Wilde deliberately focused on, and deleted, references to art, many of which alter the essay for the precision of certain argumentative points. For instance, “art of a country” was struck for “culture of a country,” while inscribing the <i>Akron</i> MS, or when Art and Life become confused during the act of crafting the art-nature mirror dynamic discussed above. This happens twice in the MS; once on <a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f035/f/f035/folio34_fluid/-1/g">f.34</a>: “They knew that <s>Art</s> <b>Life</b> gains from <s>Life</s> <b>Art</b>” (Figure 2) and the again when Wilde explicitly invokes the relationship: “Art Life holds the mirror up to Art,” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f042/f/f042/folio41_fluid/-1/g">f.41</a>).</p>
<img src="https://i.imgur.com/muE5uNM.jpeg" style="max-width:100%; height:auto; display:block; margin:0 auto;" alt="f.34 of The Decay of Lying">
<p style="text-align: center;">Figure 2. <a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f035/f/f035/folio34_fluid/-1/g">f.34</a> of “The Decay of Lying,” University of Akron: Herman Muehlstein Rare Book Collection. PR5818.d43 1889</p>
<p>Although a number of these moments see a reinsertion of the term in the immediate vicinity, the frequent deletions of “art,” especially during StBa (revisions to the <i>Akron</i> text), indicate a significant reworking of those portions of the essay engaging directly with “art.” While the initial inclination would be to suggest that this is a product of “art” being one of the most frequently appearing words in the text, this logic notably does not hold for the other high frequency terms like “nature.” Rather, “nature” was the most frequently added word, especially for those incorporated during Stage D (for the 1891 edition), with 16 additions. This contrast increased the ratio of words like “nature” and “life” in proportion to references to “art” over the course of the “life” of the work. </p> 
<p>Closer inspection of the 16 additions of nature in Stage D shows that 11 of these instances arise in extended passage on Impressionist Art, in which Vivian asserts that it is “Impressionists” who give us the “effect” of Nature, rather than Nature providing the form for the effect of the impressionists. The contention in his aesthetic philosophy that art gives form to our perception of nature, as discussed above, is one that received extensive elaboration over the course of his revisions. Not only does he make this apparent the wholly new passage on the Impressionists, but elsewhere, adding that “The only effects that she can show us are effects that we have already seen through poetry, or in paintings. This is the secret of Nature&#x27;s charm, as well as the explanation of Nature&#x27;s weakness” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f042/f/f042/folio41_fluid/-1/g">f.41</a>) and</p>
<div class="callout callout-quote"><blockquote>if you desire to see a Japanese effect, you will not behave like a tourist and go to Tokio. On the contrary, you will stay at home, and steep yourself in the work of certain Japanese artists, and then, when you have absorbed the spirit of their style, and caught their imaginative manner of vision, you will go some afternoon and sit in the Park, or stroll down Piccadilly, and if you cannot see an absolutely Japanese effect there, you will not see it anywhere. (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f046/f/f046/folio45_fluid/-1/g">f.45</a>)</blockquote></div>
<p>Wilde is clearly concerned with the distinction between nature and art and, like his removal of explicit invocation of “art,” systematically eliminates any moments when they become conflated. For example, on <a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f032/f/f032/folio31_fluid/-1/g">f.31</a>, “botanist” is replaced for “forests” and “museum” for woodland, further solidifying the natural imagery, while in the MS, “perfection” is replaced for “resemblance.”<sup><a href="#note-4" id="ref-note-4">4</a></sup> These efforts suggest that Wilde’s aesthetics are specifically an attack on mimetic Art, an attempt to recapture nature in art. This desire manifests in his elimination of references to “photographs” at sequential stages. At Stage D, he eliminates the reference to the “a photograph of a Japanese gentleman or lady,” leaving only “beside a real Japanese gentleman or lady,” which, in turn, eliminates the sense that photography (as an art) is a representation or an approximation of nature or life. It also removes the possibility that the Realist and Naturalist Arts he takes issue with in the essay is actually a self-reflexive remediation of art, as opposed to a mimetic project. Instead, as a passage from the Berg MS that was not included in the <i>Akron</i> MS suggests, Wilde considered photography and Naturalism to be (erringly) striving after the same mimetic ends: “Realism and abandoning the wicked tents of those peripatetic photographers who call them-selves Naturalistic novelists” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f030/f/f030/folio29_fluid/-1/g">f.29</a>). Indeed, the seemingly increased precision with which Wilde delineates the type of Art he is critiquing extends to his meaning of lying. Realism, in its mimetic but fictional efforts, is “lying with a moral purpose,” an attempt to replicate Life in fiction, which, with its deceitful intentions (hence, the revision to Odysseus’s “<i>sly</i> words of devising” <a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f051/f/f051/folio49_fluid/-1/g">f.49</a>), betrays an attempt to recreate or achieve something, rather than: “the only form of Lying that is absolutely beyond reproach is Lying for its own sake, and the highest development of this is, as we have already pointed out, Lying in Art.” Again pointing towards the self-reflexivity discussed above, Wilde’s three additions of “self-conscious”—sixty percent of its five total occurrences—emphasize that the highest form of art should be a “<i>self-conscious creation</i>” and not any sort of deluded attempt replicate, remediate, or <i>re</i>present.</p>
<p>The “effect” of these revisions (and Wilde was increasingly preoccupied with “effects”,—perhaps the structuring effects of art—quadrupling its frequency from 3 to 13 between 1889 and 1891) in the end, is an increasingly harsh critique of Realism, mimetic art, and Nature in a fashion that amplifies the two prongs of Wilde’s theory of art, as Hilda Schiff describes them: that “we degrade art by asking it to follow nature” and that “good art not only does not follow nature, but nature often appears to mirror art. Believing that things exist only in so far as they are perceived, man&#x27;s perceptions, when loaded with art-images, project upon nature an existence imitative of art” (90). While the latter is articulated in the MS draft in passages like the concluding lines of <a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f055/f/f055/folio53_fluid/-1/g">f.53</a>: “The third doctrine is that Life imitates Art far more than Art imitates Life. This results not merely from Life&#x27;s imitative instinct, but from the fact that the self-conscious aim of Life is to find expression, and that Art offers it certain beautiful forms through which it may realize that energy” and in several revisions, like the Stage D addition just prior to this assertion: “Life goes faster than Realism, but Romanticism is always in front of Life” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f055/f/f055/folio53_fluid/-1/g">f.53</a>) and the above additions that make the claim explicit (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f055/f/f055/folio53_fluid/-1/g">f.53</a>).</p>
<p>However, the former of Schiff’s assertions, and the volatility of “degradation” as opposed to err or fail, might be valuably explored through a form of computational literary studies: sentiment analysis.</p>
<p>A form of Natural Language Procession (NLP), sentiment analysis is a type of text classification which categorizes the words in a text or texts by emotion and sentiment, and in some algorithms assigns a quantitative value according to its emotional valence.<sup><a href="#note-5" id="ref-note-5">5</a></sup> When calculating the sentiment of a corpus, “sentiment analysis maps specific words [also referred to as tokens] to specific sentiment values. These values, which are looked up in a sentiment dictionary (or “lexicon”), range from positive to negative according to the specific design of the dictionary” (Jockers 160).  But since in sentiment analysis “the focus is turned away from the actual [content], and more toward the author’s presentation or organization” of the text, joining this analysis with encoded revisions enables investigation into the shifts in the kinds of textual presentation, the style and tone of the language introduced, removed, and reworked (Jockers 160).<sup><a href="#note-6" id="ref-note-6">6</a></sup> This unconventional method of literary research can reveal hitherto overlooked dynamics and, even when it fails to accurately capture the nuances of language, highlights textual complexities that can be analyzed through close reading. By situating scholars in a defamiliarized position to the textual condition, sentiment analysis and other CLS methods can bring to the fore the rich intricacies at the syntactical and even lexical level. By offering a methodology for studying textual fluidity within the context of creative ecologies and concurrence, such approaches promise to raise important new questions and insights into the complex relationship between material reading practices and composition.</p>
<p>Considering Wilde’s characteristic affectation, alongside the essay’s aesthetic preoccupation with effect, presentation, impression, and <i>art</i>ifice, sentiment analysis’s measuring the emotional volatility of language positions it as a form of CLS particularly well-suited, if also counter-intuitive, for exploring the semantic effect of the fluid variations of “The Decay of Lying.” It offers in its calculations a way of (not infallibly) measuring the effect of the changes to presentation, throwing into stark relief how even the most minute alterations in diction can drastically alter the impression of the text, even if not its “substance.” Additionally, the aesthetes’ contentions prize the artists’ <i>subjective</i> sense of truth:
<div class="callout callout-quote"><blockquote>the writer's aim, consciously or unconsciously, comes to be the <i>transcribing,</i> not of the world, not of mere fact, but of his sense of it, he becomes an artist, his work fine art; and good art in proportion to the truth of his presentment of that sense. . . The transcript of his sense of fact rather than the fact, as being preferable, pleasanter, more beautiful to the writer himself</blockquote></div> 

<br>This deeply personal and variable understanding of impersonality, “this transcript of fact. . .in its <i>infinite variety</i>, modified by human preference in all its infinitely varied forms,” interestingly complicates sentiment analysis’s ostensibly “objective” quantitative measures of sentiment or “effect” (Pater 9-11, emphasis added). Nonetheless, the aesthetes’ attention to precision in “translat[ing] from inward to outward. . .some personal sense of fact” is chiefly preoccupied with the minuteness of expression and the most subtle nuances of language (34), since “all beauty is in the long run only fineness of truth, or <i>what we call expression, the finer accommodation of speech to that vision within</i>” (10, emphasis added), is similarly brought to the fore and centered as the object of inquiry in sentiment analysis. The methodology, aims, and even fallibility of sentiment analysis suggest that as a <i>complementary</i> method for studying the aesthetic theories (and the self-reflexive praxis of those theories in crictal essays), it may prove valuable in orienting an insightful critical inquiry. Indeed, through sentiment analysis, in both the passages and words it calls attention to, and in the moments where it clearly fails, highlights the aesthetes’ belief “that every language possesses a genius, a very fastidious genius, of its own” (15). And sentiment analysis, in prompting new and reoriented close readings through the results it returns, its empirical and quantitative relationship to the text, and how both situates scholars in defamiliarized position, can aid in generating new research questions and avenues into the text, especially those like Wilde’s dealing primarily themselves the ambiguity and volatility of literary theory and movements. </p> 
<p>When performing a sentiment analysis on both 1889 and 1891 variants, and tracking how that changes over the course of the text, a clear divergence emerges at what was once the nadir of the 1889 periodical version (Figure 3).  The low point in the <i>Nineteenth Century</i> variant is the Vivian’s recounting of the “Hyde” like incident, upon revision, with extremely “negative” words like “horror,” “terror,” “frightened,” “scream” and “rough people” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f038/f/f038/folio37_fluid/-1/g">f.37</a>).<sup><a href="#note-7" id="ref-note-7">7</a></sup> However, upon revision, Wilde flattens the emotional volatility of the scene by adding “well-written” and “humanitarian,” and through the inclusion of more positive language in the paragraph that follows (words like “beauty,” “great,” and “friend”).</p>
<img src="https://i.imgur.com/VNP21m0.jpeg" style="max-width:100%; height:auto; display:block; margin:0 auto;" alt="Plot of Rolling Mean of Syuzhet Sentiment Analysis Scores of 1889 and 1891 editions">
<p style="text-align: center;">Figure 3. Plot of Rolling Mean of Syuzhet Sentiment Analysis Scores of 1889 and 1891 editions.</p>
<p>However, in <i>Intentions</i>, there is a precipitous fall, about 75% of the way through the essay, that’s not present in the <i>Nineteenth Century</i> variant. This is a result of the insertion of the extensive passage deriding the Nature as a derivative of Art, and lambasting any who turn idolize Nature. the “degradation” that Schiff associates with part of Wilde’s aesthetic principle becomes measurably apparent in the cluster of the second half of the added passage rife with “negative” language, which becomes the most negative point of the second half of the essay; passages, like  “It was simply a very second-rate Turner, a Turner of a bad period, with all the painter&#x27;s worst faults exaggerated and over-emphasized,” “She produces her false Renés and her sham Vautrins, just as Nature gives us, on one day a doubtful Cuyp, and on another a more than questionable Rousseau. Still, Nature irritates one more when she does things of that kind. It seems so stupid, so obvious, so unnecessary.” and “A doubtful Cuyp is unbearable” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f042/f/f042/folio41_fluid/-1/g">f.41</a>). This added passage typifies a tendency for revisions to increase the “negative” presentation of Wilde’s criticisms and to redirect/redeploy the “negative” sentiments in his text. For instance, Wilde inserts” replaces “healthy national feeling” with “solemn depressing truths” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f026/f/f026/folio25_fluid/-1/g">f.25</a>), “allowed” for “suffered” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f024/f/f024/folio23_fluid/-1/g">f.23</a>), “bad” for “depressing” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f019/f/f019/folio18_fluid/-1/g">f.18</a>), “After all,” for “It is a humiliating confession” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f014/f/f014/folio13_fluid/-1/g">f.13</a>),<sup><a href="#note-8" id="ref-note-8">8</a></sup> and “probable” for “tedious” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f049/f/f049/folio48a_fluid/-1/g">f.48</a>; Table 1). On <a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f049/f/f049/folio48a_fluid/-1/g">f.48</a>, “sordid” was added when drafting the <i>Akron</i> MS, “doomed to absolute oblivion” was added for the 1891 edition,<sup><a href="#note-9" id="ref-note-9">9</a></sup> and making “Sleep” the deceptive agent acting upon us, “play[ing] us false,” is a revision of the Berg variant in which Sleep is the victim of cultural influences: “Sleep herself has not escaped the baneful influences of our time” (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f049/f/f049/folio48a_fluid/-1/g">f.48</a>). While there are, of course, sporadic instances in which “positive” language is added in revisions, like his added praise of the art that life imitates: Stevenson’s “well-written” story,<sup><a href="#note-10" id="ref-note-10">10</a></sup> these also work to lower the negative “sentiment” in passages that align with Vivian’s principles (here, Stevenson’s story prefigures and anticipates “life”—or “it should”).</p> 
<p>Sentiment analysis of revisions, in calling attention to how the presentation of the interlocuters' discussion changes (for example, Cyril’s “Is that the end of this dangerous the article” is changed to “I like it. Is that the end?”). And, on the whole, the quantitative results point to key revisions to magnify or add negative language to sharpen the essay’s critique while homing in on the type of art it targets, like the criticisms of Hall Caine, James Payn, and William Black added to the <i>Intentions</i> version of the essay (<a href="https://brandonhurst01.github.io/The-Decay-of-Lying-2025/#/ec/f011/f/f011/folio10_intentions1891/-1/g?zone=%23f055_z001">f.10</a>). Calculating the average sentiment score for each sentence of the 1889 and 1891 variants, indicates that the later variant has on average more “negatively” connotated language, reflecting the honing hostility of the essay’s rhetoric across variants that can be observed in individual isolations. And, mirroring the refinement of diction that clarifies the type of art to be praised, and the type to be critiqued, the revised criticisms are not only amplified in their emotional valence, but they are also specified to narrow and more precisely defines the “imitative arts” it critiques. Thus, in a very Wildean and Paterian fashion, sentiment analysis suggests that across the fluid, living texts, the characteristics of the linguistic <i>presentation</i>, the subject, and the concepts of the argument increasingly align: “The term is right, and has its essential beauty, when it becomes, in a manner, what it signifies, as with the names of simple sensations” (22).</p>
