		<h3 id="notes">Notes <a href="#notes" class="anchor">🔗</a></h3>
			<p id="note-1">
  <a href="#ref-note-1">1.</a>
				Bristow and Mitchell document the string of sales as follows: “Stuart Mason [Christopher Sclater Millard] recorded its sale at Sotheby’s for 111 on July 26, 1910” after it had been given by Wilde to “Mr Frank Richardson” (see also Stuart Mason [Christopher Millard], <i>Bibliography of Oscar Wilde</i> (London, 1914), 123). They then note that “In 1920, an ‘original manuscript of ‘‘The Decay of Lying’’, written on fifty-five pages folio’ was purchased by distinguished rare book collector Dr. A. S. W. Rosenbach for $1,525. It later appeared in the Rosenbach Company’s 1933 sale catalogue, listed at $3,400. The manuscript reappeared in 1937, when, during a recession that halted the post-Depression recovery, the <i>New York Times</i> recorded ‘an autograph manuscript of Oscar Wilde’s ‘‘The Decay of Lying’’, containing about 12,500 words, went to Retz &amp; Storm for $975’ at an auction at the American Art Association’s Anderson Galleries.” How it “became part of the collection of rubber magnate Herman Muehlstein” is not reported, but “in 1957, the University of Akron awarded Muehlstein, President of H. Muehlstein and Co., dealers of plastics and rubber, an Honorary Doctorate of Humane Letters. Two years earlier, Muehlstein had donated a first round of items from his rare book collection to the university and pledged to donate the remainder upon his death. In 1962, the remainder of the bequest, including the fair copy of Wilde’s ‘The Decay of Lying’, entered Akron’s collection.” (573–74).
				<br><br>
				Links to announcements of these sales can be found here:<br>
