      <p id="note-2">
  <a href="#ref-note-2">2.</a>
  See the substitution of “a third-class railway carriage” for “an Omnibus” in the <i>Akron</i> MS (f.24) and the reference to North London, which in Charles Booth’s Map, were color coded black meaning “Lowest class, Vicious, Semi-criminal” <a href="https://booth.lse.ac.uk/map/browse" target="_blank">https://booth.lse.ac.uk/map/browse</a>.
</p>

<p id="note-3">
  <a href="#ref-note-3">3.</a>
  For example, Wilde similar deploys, but inverts, the material mirroring over the line break to level a critique. On f.48b, Wilde inscribed:<br>
   <blockquote>
    in the English Church a man succeeds,<br>
    not in proper through his capacity for<br>
    belief, but through his capacity for<br>
    disbelief.
  </blockquote>
The very asymmetry between the Church’s practice and doctrine is itself mirrored as “belief” and “disbelief” are mirrored over the line break. <br>
Likewise in his attack on the “English melodrama,” this line break mirroring appears once more, but this time to criticize a an erroneous or ill-advised mirroring of dialogue “on” and “off” the stage:<br>
<blockquote>
   Their characters in <b>these plays</b> talk
on the stage exactly as they would talk
off it; <b>they have neither aspirations nor aspirates;</b> (f.24)
  </blockquote>
  The problematic of the melodrama taking “directly from life and reproduce[ing] its vulgarity down to the smallest detail” is materially reflected and instantiated in the inscription on the page.
</p>
<p id="note-4">
  <a href="#ref-note-4">4.</a>
  One is tempted here to also point to the change from “rose” to “grew” on f.49, but the hand behind the change is unknown. 
</p>

<p id="note-5">
  <a href="#ref-note-5">5.</a>
Emotional valence is the spectrum of positive and negative connotations of language used.</p>

<p id="note-6">
  <a href="#ref-note-6">6.</a>
  The NRC lexicon has 8 basic emotions in which a word can be categorized, as well as “positive” or “negative” association. "The Bing lexicon[4] has a binary categorization, which simply has two values of –1 and 1. The Afinn lexicon [5] grades words between –5 and 5. The Syuzhet lexicon has more specific values for each sentiment word, ranging between –1 and 1, which are –1.0, –0.8 –0.75, –0.6, –0.5, –0.4, –0.25, 0.1, 0.25, 0.4, 0.5, 0.6, 0.75, 0.8, 1.0.” (Kim 2). The present analysis uses the Syuzhet lexicon, developed by the Nebraska Literary Lab under the direction of Matthew Jockers, and draws upon the work of computational linguists at The Stanford NLP Group (Jockers 161).
See: 
• Jockers, Matthew L. Text Analysis with R for Students of Literature. 2nd ed. Cham: Springer Nature, 2020. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-03164-4.  
• Jockers, M. L. Blog comment on “Problems with the Syuzhet Package.” Anglophile in Academia: Annie Swafford’s Blog. (2015). https://annieswafford.wordpress.com/2015/03/02/syuzhet/comment-page-1/#comment-54. 
• Jockers, M. L. “Introduction to the Syuzhet Package.” (2017). https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/syuzhet/vignettes/syuzhet-vignette.html. 
• Jockers, M. L. “Syuzhet Package v1.0.7.” R Documentation. (August 11th, 2023). https://www.rdocumentation.org/packages/syuzhet/versions/1.0.4.\ 

</p>

<p id="note-7">
  <a href="#ref-note-7">7.</a>
  In a fascinating removal in the MS, Wilde cuts “looking” from “rough looking people” turning a subjective perception into an aesthetic judgment that assumes the subjective perception of “rough” is an inherent quality of the object “people.” This single cut collapses the distinction between perception, informed by Art (here, Stevenson’s story), and the “fact” or “truth” of reality. 
</p>

<p id="note-8">
  <a href="#ref-note-8">8.</a>
 Also on f.13, “horrible” is substituted for “terrible,” only to then be replaced later for “depressing and humiliating”
